#---
# purpose: "two Enum.reduce/3 users over different element types publish their own returns, not one joined return"
# oracle: oracle.exs
#---
#
# fz-kdt.122's oracle, and fz-kdt.183's keystone gate.
#
# `List.reduce_step/3` dispatches only on its accumulator tag, so its own entry
# mask leaves the list slot `Ignore` and the recursive collapse used to erase
# the element: one activation stood for BOTH `Enum.reduce/3` users here, and
# the one return it published was the join of an `[int]` and a `[binary]`. The
# int user then saw a `binary` in its accumulator and `+/2` failed its @spec.
#
# `reduce_step/3` FORWARDS its list to `List.reduce_cont/3`, whose key IS
# ground in the element, so the element decides which callee activation the
# forward reaches and therefore what comes back. fz-kdt.183 joins that demand
# into the key: `[list(int), {:cont, list(a1_1_e)}, a2]` beside
# `[list(binary), {:cont, list(a1_1_e)}, a2]`, two activations, two returns.
# The accumulator payload and the reducer stay free -- only the demanded
# element is kept.
#
# The flipped twin `reduce_users_key_apart_by_element_flipped.fz` is the same
# program with the two statements swapped: at HEAD the published return
# depended on which user was analysed first, so the pair is what says the
# answer is the program's and not the schedule's.
fn main() do
  dbg(Enum.reduce([1, 2], [], fn (x, acc) -> [x + 200 | acc] end))
  dbg(Enum.reduce(["a", "b"], [], fn (x, acc) -> [x <> "!" | acc] end))
end
